#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056036 :tutorial/content "A **set** is a collection of objects known as **elements** or **members**.\n\nUsually, the elements of a set have some property in common. For example, a set could be a class at school where the students are the members of the set. The common property is that all the students are members of the class.\n\nTo write a set, we list the elements between two braces (curly brackets). Commas are used to separate the elements.\n\nSo, for example, the set of the primary colors would be written as\n\n$$\nS =\\{ \\text{yellow}, \\: \\text{red}, \\: \\text{blue} \\}.\n$$\n\nThe elements of this set are $\\text{yellow},$ $\\text{red},$ and $\\text{blue}.$\n\nA set that does not contain any elements is known as the **null set** or **empty set** and is written as either\n\n$$\n\\emptyset \\quad  \\text{or} \\quad \\{\\}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- *A set may contain other sets as elements.* For instance, the set\n\n$$\nT = \\{1, \\: 2, \\: 3, \\: 4, \\: \\{5, 6\\}\\}\n$$\n\ncontains the set $\\{5, 6\\}$ as an element. However, it does *not* contain the elements $5$ and $6$.\n\n- *Order doesn't matter.* The order in which the elements are listed is irrelevant. For instance, the following sets represent the same thing:\n\n$$\nA = \\{1, \\: 2, \\: 3 \\} = \\{2, \\: 3, \\: 1\\}\n$$\n\nIt's usually easier to understand a set if the elements are listed in some order.\n\n- *Duplicates don't matter.* Having multiple copies of an element does not affect the structure of a set. For example, the following sets represent the same thing:\n\n$$\nB = \\{m, \\: n, \\: p, \\: q\\} = \\{m, \\: m, \\: m, \\: n, \\: n, \\: p, \\: p, \\: p, \\: q\\}\n$$\n\nWhen writing a set, we should try to organize it so that each element is listed once only." :tutorial/id #uuid "d42ef3b1-d046-5e5d-b671-e5bb3eee9963" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8211 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056037 :tutorial/content "By convention, sets are denoted by uppercase letters, while lowercase letters refer to elements of the set.  \n\nThere is a special notation, $\\in,$ for expressing elements of sets. For example, if $a$ is an element of a set $A,$ we write\n\n$$\na \\in A.\n$$\n\nFor example, for the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\{ \\text{yellow}, \\text{red}, \\text{blue} \\},\n$$\n\nwe can write \n\n$$\n\\text{yellow}\\in S.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $a$ is *not* an element of $A,$ then we write\n\n$$\na \\notin A.\n$$\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\text{green} \\notin S.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2d5957fc-01d1-567b-8834-752b6a1b99a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8212 :tutorial/title "Element Notation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056038 :tutorial/content "When a set contains only a few elements, we can write them all explicitly. \n\nFor example, the set $A$ containing all positive even integers less than $20$ could be written as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\{2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: 8, \\: 10, \\: 12, \\: 14, \\: 16, \\: 18\\}\n$$\n\nHowever, when a set contains many elements, we can save ourselves the pain of writing out all its elements using an ellipsis, \"$\\ldots$\". \n\nFor example:\n\n- The set $B$ containing all positive even integers less than $200$ can be written as\n\n$$\nB = \\{2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: \\ldots, \\: 198\\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ containing *all* positive even integers can be written as\n\n$$\nC = \\{ 2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: \\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\nGenerally, an ellipsis means \"and so on up to\" if it's followed by a specific element or \"and so on\" if not.\n\nFinally, note that $A$ and $B$ contain a finite number of elements ($A$ and $B$ contain $9$ and $99$ elements, respectively), while $C$ contains infinitely many elements:\n\n- A set containing a finite number of elements is called a **finite set**.\n\n- A set containing an infinite number of elements is called an **infinite set**.\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ are finite sets, while $C$ is an infinite set." :tutorial/id #uuid "eda65aa9-454a-53a2-a1cd-d218730af2b5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8213 :tutorial/title "Ellipsis Notation"}]}
